WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A sustainable Moodle LMS site is operated through a lifecycle.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:15.720
The work begins with planning: who the site serves, which programs are critical, what data is stored, which teams own decisions, and what availability and recovery expectations apply.

3
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:28.300
Installation and baseline configuration then turn those decisions into a platform that has verified scheduled processing, mail delivery, protected storage, backups, monitoring, support ownership, and documented defaults.

4
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.940
The next part of the lifecycle is structure and access.

5
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:42.440
Categories, course defaults, naming standards, course request processes, enrolment methods, and lifecycle rules determine whether the site can be governed at scale.

6
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:53.260
Authentication proves identity; enrolment places that identity into courses; roles and capabilities define what the person can do; contexts define where that authority applies.

7
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.120
Least privilege makes those decisions safer and easier to review.

8
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.520
Courses and plugins require ongoing governance.

9
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:07.260
Course copy, import, backup, and restore serve different purposes and carry different data consequences.

10
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.560
Completion data can support records, but it is not automatic proof of competence.

11
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:24.020
Plugins and integrations add value when they solve real problems with clear ownership, compatibility review, privacy review, test evidence, support expectations, and removal plans.

12
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.160
They create risk when they become unowned dependencies.

13
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.480
Operations keep the site trustworthy.

14
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.300
Cron and scheduled tasks must run.

15
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.120
Email and notifications must be diagnosable.

16
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.820
Logs and reports must be interpreted with humility.

17
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:46.480
Security settings reduce risk in layers, while privacy tools support governance without guaranteeing compliance.

18
00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.220
Backups must be protected and monitored, but recovery must be tested.

19
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.240
Performance work should be based on measurements instead of hunches.

20
00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.160
Upgrades and incidents reveal whether administration is mature.

21
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:12.580
A safe upgrade verifies requirements, checks plugins and themes, rehearses in staging, confirms backups, plans rollback, communicates maintenance, validates behavior, and monitors afterward.

22
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:22.280
An incident response needs owners, escalation, evidence, communication, recovery procedures, and a review after the event.

23
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.620
These activities are normal administration, not exceptional paperwork.

24
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.620
The administrator's final habit is improvement.

25
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:43.000
Review support trends, failed tasks, restore-test outcomes, role exceptions, plugin health, privacy requests, security notices, performance data, and course lifecycle problems.

26
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:50.220
Then update documentation, adjust defaults, refine support, retire stale assets, and plan the next change.

27
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.600
A working Moodle site is only the starting point.

28
00:02:53.700 --> 00:03:00.260
A well-operated Moodle site is owned, measured, governed, recoverable, and continually improved.

29
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.760
That is the difference between maintaining software and operating a learning service.
